# Line endings are checked in with LF and are checked out with LF, on every
# platform including Windows.
#
# This file exists because of a concrete CI failure. GitHub's windows-latest
# image configures `core.autocrlf=true`, so `actions/checkout` wrote every file
# into the working tree with CRLF line endings. Nothing was actually
# misformatted, but `gofmt` reads CRLF as a formatting difference, so
# `gofmt -l internal/ cmd/` reported all 96 .go files and the `gofmt` step of
# all three `windows-latest` legs failed on every run:
#
#     ##[error]gofmt reported files that are not formatted
#     ##[error]run 'gofmt -w internal\app\app.go'
#     ... one per file
#
# That is worse than a red build: the step could no longer catch a genuine
# formatting failure on Windows, because every file always looked unformatted.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides the runner's `core.autocrlf`, so the checkout is LF there
# too and the check means the same thing on all three operating systems. The
# attributes below agree with `.editorconfig`, which editors already honour;
# this file is what makes git agree with it.

# Default for everything not matched below: let git detect text vs binary (the
# repository tracks no binaries, so this is text in practice), but always check
# text out with LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Go source is called out as well as covered by the rule above, because it is
# the one extension the `gofmt` CI step depends on and should not be at the
# mercy of a later edit to the default rule.
*.go text eol=lf

# The module files are matched byte for byte against the checksum database, so
# a stray CRLF here would change every hash in go.sum.
go.mod text eol=lf
go.sum text eol=lf
